{
  "format": "optibench-workstation",
  "version": 1,
  "meta": {
    "appVersion": "0.1.0-alpha",
    "author": "Optibench examples",
    "created": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "description": "Analog optical link with a transimpedance receiver. TRANSMITTER: the function generator (5 Hz sine, 4.2 V +/- 0.6 V) drives a 650 nm laser diode through 47 ohm, always above its 20 mA threshold, so the emitted power follows the sine (about 8 to 26 mW); an EMITTER link makes it the power of the bench laser. OPTICS: an OD 2 neutral-density filter attenuates the beam 100x before it reaches the photodiode head, whose reading a DETECTOR link feeds to the circuit photodiode PD1 (0.5 A/W). RECEIVER: the TL072 op-amp U1 holds the photodiode at 0 V (virtual ground) and converts its photocurrent into a voltage across RF: V = I x 47 k (about 2 to 6 V); CF keeps the loop stable. The LM393 comparator U2 compares that with the threshold set on RV1 (about 3.3 V) and lights the SIGNAL LED through its open-collector output whenever the light level is above it. Probes TX and RX show the laser drive and the recovered signal; block the beam or change the filter density and watch RX collapse. Supply: two 9 V batteries as a +/-9 V split rail.",
    "dimension": "2d",
    "modified": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "title": "Op-Amp Photodiode Amplifier"
  },
  "optics": {
    "components": [
